	affiliations = {SIES Graduate School of Technology, Department of Electronics and Telecommunication, Navi Mumbai, India},
	abstract = {Current vending machines are widespread in public spaces, offering a range of products through visual interfaces and traditional payment methods, such as coins and cards. However, they often lack accessibility features for visually impaired users, who face difficulties navigating menus independently. Additionally, many machines support only a single language, limiting usability for non-native speakers. To address these shortcomings, we propose a Multilingual Voice Based Vending Machine (VBVM) that enhances accessibility and usability. Our system operates via voice commands, enabling visually impaired users to interact with the machine without physical assistance. It supports four languages, ensuring inclusivity for non-native speakers, and includes braille on the keypads for additional accessibility. The VBVM also incorporates flexible payment methods, including both coin-based transactions and cashless payments through the Unified Payments Interface (UPI). By providing a user-friendly, voice-enabled interface and multiple accessibility features, the VBVM aims to offer an inclusive vending experience for all users, regardless of their visual or linguistic capabilities. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
