	abstract = {Digital technology is developing rapidly every day; therefore, it can't be denied that it will reach the financial sector. But payment with cash still the first option for the payment method in Indonesia. There are 92% of transactions instore are using cash as the payment method every day. The presence of cashless as a payment method enables wider access to a much efficient payment system for users. The large population of the millennial generation is a challenge as well as an opportunity for mobile wallet business players in Indonesia, considering millennials behavior can encourage them to be the key to driving Indonesia's economy. Jakarta as capital city of Indonesia as the epicentrum of business. Therefore, this research discusses how to identify factors that form use behavior on using the mobile wallet for Millennial's generation in DKI Jakarta, Indonesia. The sample of this research focused on millennials e-wallet user of the top 5 mobile wallet applications in Indonesia namely Go-Pay, OVO, DANA, LinkAja and Jenius. This research proposed a model based on UTAUT and UTAUT2. Based on the questionnaire distribution, there are 469 respondent's data obtained and analysed using statistics descriptive variable and structural equation modelling (SEM). The result of the hypothesis test reveals that the performance expectations, effort expectations, social influences, facilitation conditions have positive effects and significant in behavioral intentions. Behavioral intention has a positive effect and significant on behavioral use. While habits and trust have shown direct influence on Behavioral intentions. © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
