	affiliations = {Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia, Faculty of Economics and Management, Bangi, Malaysia; Norwich Business School, Norwich, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Facial recognition payment (FRP) is a new-age technology that empowers the cashless society, who may be physical or virtual customers of marketplaces and visitors to countries accepting FRP. Despite the potential of FRP to contribute to sustainable development (e.g., mitigate physical contact and reduce the amount of energy and water to produce cash, and thus, contribute to better environmental and public health), the success of FRP inarguably depends on its continuance use intention, a topic that remains underexplored in information systems. Considering that behaviours are formed sequentially, this study adopts the cognitive appraisal theory and develops a model that includes primary and secondary appraisals alongside the moderating effect of perceived technology security to theories the continuance use intention of FRP. Usable data was collected from 350 respondents with FRP experiences through an online survey and analysed using PLS-SEM. Taken collectively, this study contributes theoretical and managerial insights for maximizing and sustaining the promise and utility of FRP. © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
