	year = {2023},
	pages = {196 - 217},
	doi = {10.4324/9781003441298-10},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85173849965&doi=10.4324%2F9781003441298-10&partnerID=40&md5=c72ae8d9c568e19464d964c2f242e5dd},
	affiliations = {Universitas Sumatera Utara, Medan, Indonesia},
	abstract = {The COVID-19 pandemic has significantly impacted the use of digital wallets in Indonesia. The trend of contactless cashless payments that offer various conveniences, efficiencies, promotions and discounts in shopping has increased the adoption of mobile payment technology. The viability of a mobile payment company is highly dependent on Continuance Intention’s relationship with users. However, competition between e-wallet providers is very tight; this can be seen from the offer of discount and cashback promotional programmes to attract consumers. Even this competition to acquire customers leads to a strategy of burning money. Unfortunately, relatively low switching costs make users easily switch service providers. Mobile payments have been proven to help businesses and micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) increase sales turnover and customer experience. For millennials, especially the unbanked segment, mobile payments and pay later options have increased impulse buying and consumerism. The survey results show that users are satisfied with mobile payment services but need better education and literacy, so millennials are wiser about spending their money. © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
