	affiliations = {Cathedral Communications Ltd, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Not until recently has there been so much talk in the press about cashless payment systems. The news that National Westminster Bank in conjunction with Midland and British Telecom will be offering their customers a revolutionary multipurpose smart money card called Mondex, suggests that plastic payments could soon replace coins and notes. Mondex will be linked in 1996 to Nat West and Midland cash-points and special BT smart phones. The card, which comes with a pocket-sized reader-writer or electronic wallet-sized keypad, will allow customers to zap money on to their cards, to and from their accounts, make instant payments over the phone and even buy a newspaper from merchants subscribing to the scheme. The shift from cash and paper currencies to electronic money on plastic, could save the banks in excess of £4.5 billion this year. It’s not surprising why they want to go cashless. © 1994, MCB UP Limited © 2016 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
